        "starterCode": "public class HelloWorld {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, World!\");\n        System.out.println(\"Entering the JVM Citadel...\");\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>☕ What is Java? JDK, JRE & The JVM Bytecode Engine</h2>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout--quest\">\n  <strong>⚔️ JAVA FORGE:</strong> <em>Welcome to the JVM Citadel. Before writing software for millions of concurrent enterprise users, you must understand how Java source code is compiled into universal bytecode and executed via the HotSpot JIT compiler.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Java achieved legendary dominance in enterprise software through its foundational promise: <strong>Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)</strong>. While languages like C/C++ compile directly to CPU-specific machine instructions (requiring recompilation for every OS and architecture), Java decouples code from bare metal through the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).</p>\n  <p>The developer writes human-readable <code>.java</code> source files. The Java Compiler (<code>javac</code>) translates this into platform-independent <strong>Bytecode</strong> (<code>.class</code> files). When your application boots, the JVM loads the bytecode, verifies security constraints, and uses the <strong>HotSpot JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler</strong> to compile frequently executed \"hot\" bytecode methods directly into native machine assembly on the fly!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Architecture</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\n[ Developer Code: Solution.java ]\n                 |\n                 v (javac Compiler)\n[ Universal Bytecode: Solution.class ]\n                 |\n                 +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                 |                       |                       |\n                 v                       v                       v\n      [ Windows x86_64 JVM ]   [ Linux ARM64 JVM ]       [ macOS M3 JVM ]\n      (HotSpot JIT -> x86 ASM) (HotSpot JIT -> ARM ASM)  (HotSpot JIT -> ARM ASM)\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Enterprise Best Practices & Production Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>JDK vs JRE:</strong> JDK (Java Development Kit) includes the compiler javac and debugging tools; JRE (Java Runtime Environment) only contains the JVM to run compiled bytecode.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tiered Compilation:</strong> HotSpot uses C1 (Client Compiler) for ultra-fast startup and C2 (Server Compiler) for aggressive runtime inlining and loop vectorization.</li>\n  <li><strong>Classloader Hierarchy:</strong> Bootstrap ClassLoader -> Platform ClassLoader -> Application ClassLoader enforces cryptographic security.</li>\n</ul>"
