        "title": "Primitive Types, 2's Complement & Overflow Runes",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "public class PrimitiveRunes {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 8 Primitive Types\n        byte smallRune = 127;\n        short mana = 32000;\n        int goldCoins = 2_147_483_647; // Max signed 32-bit int\n        long galacticScore = 9_000_000_000L;\n\n        float spellSpeed = 3.14f;\n        double preciseCoordinates = 124.5678912;\n        char elementalGlyph = '⚡';\n        boolean isShieldActive = true;\n\n        System.out.println(\"Max Int: \" + goldCoins);\n        // Overflow demon: adding 1 wraps to negative minimum!\n        System.out.println(\"Overflowed Int: \" + (goldCoins + 1));\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>☕ Primitive Types, 2's Complement & Numeric Overflow Runes</h2>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout--quest\">\n  <strong>⚔️ JAVA FORGE:</strong> <em>Bits and bytes forge the universe. Understand Java's 8 primitive types, two's complement binary representation, and how unchecked arithmetic wraps silently around boundaries.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Java provides 8 built-in <strong>Primitive Types</strong> allocated directly on the stack without object overhead: <code>byte</code> (8-bit), <code>short</code> (16-bit), <code>int</code> (32-bit), <code>long</code> (64-bit), <code>float</code> (32-bit IEEE 754), <code>double</code> (64-bit IEEE 754), <code>boolean</code>, and <code>char</code> (16-bit UTF-16).</p>\n  <p>All signed integer primitives are represented using <strong>Two's Complement</strong> binary. When an integer exceeds <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code> (2,147,483,647), adding 1 flips the sign bit, causing it to silently wrap around to <code>Integer.MIN_VALUE</code> (-2,147,483,648)!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Architecture</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\n32-Bit Signed Integer Boundary:\n  01111111 11111111 11111111 11111111 = +2,147,483,647 (Integer.MAX_VALUE)\n+                                   1\n-------------------------------------\n  10000000 00000000 00000000 00000000 = -2,147,483,648 (Integer.MIN_VALUE! OVERFLOW!)\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Enterprise Best Practices & Production Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Safe Midpoint Calculation:</strong> Never write (left + right) / 2 in binary search; when left and right are large, they overflow into negative numbers! Use left + (right - left) / 2.</li>\n  <li><strong>Math.addExact():</strong> Java 8+ provides Math.addExact() and multiplyExact() which throw ArithmeticException instead of silently corrupting data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Floating-Point Imprecision:</strong> Never use float or double for financial currency calculations; always use java.math.BigDecimal!</li>\n</ul>"
