        "title": "Constructors, Chaining (this()) & Immutable Records",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "public class ConstructorDemo {\n    record QuestItem(String name, int rarity, int power) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        QuestItem sword = new QuestItem(\"Sunblade\", 5, 120);\n        System.out.println(sword.name() + \" | Power: \" + sword.power());\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>☕ Constructors, Chaining (this()) & Immutable Records</h2>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout--quest\">\n  <strong>⚔️ JAVA FORGE:</strong> <em>Eliminate boilerplate with modern Java Records! Understand constructor chaining with this(), canonical constructors, and compact record validations.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Constructors establish valid initial state. When multiple overloaded constructors exist, use <strong>Constructor Chaining (<code>this(...)</code>)</strong> to delegate to a single primary constructor, avoiding redundant initialization logic.</p>\n  <p>In modern Java 16+, <strong>Records</strong> eliminate hundreds of lines of boilerplate for immutable data carrier classes. A record automatically generates private final fields, a canonical constructor, getters, <code>equals()</code>, <code>hashCode()</code>, and <code>toString()</code> in a single line: <code>public record Hero(String name, int level) {}</code>.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Architecture</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nTraditional Class (45 lines):      Modern Java Record (1 line!):\npublic class Hero {               public record Hero(String name, int level) {\n  private final String name;        // Compact validation constructor:\n  private final int level;          public Hero {\n  public Hero(name, level) {...}      if (level < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n  public String name() {...}        }\n  public boolean equals() {...}   }\n  public int hashCode() {...}\n}\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Enterprise Best Practices & Production Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Compact Constructor:</strong> Records allow compact validation constructors without repeating parameter assignments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shallow Immutability:</strong> Records are shallowly immutable; if a record holds a mutable List, the list itself can still be modified unless wrapped in List.copyOf().</li>\n  <li><strong>No Subclassing:</strong> Records are implicitly final and extend java.lang.Record; they cannot extend other classes.</li>\n</ul>"
